// case: at-on-list-of-strings
// what: the most ordinary list program there is, and it answers 0. n10 is the
//      same defect on a list of ints; this is the one a user actually writes,
//      because a list of strings is what a beginner reaches for first.
//      `isStringReg` and its two siblings (`compiler/c_backend.orb:2052-2056`)
//      classify the receiver by name and shape with no type, take the string
//      branch, and emit `orbit_string_at` on a list pointer -- a string
//      function called on an OrbitList. Measured: `s.at(1)` over
//      ["alpha","beta","gamma"] prints 0, and it should print "beta". Stable
//      across runs, so the wrong value is pinned.
//
//      Note what is NOT wrong here: `s.len()` is 3, and `s.at(1)` on a real
//      string is correct. Only `.at()` is broken, and only on a list, which is
//      exactly why nothing else in the suite notices. FINDINGS F-0002.
// known-defect: F-0002
// wrong-value: 0
fn main() -> int {
    val s = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]
    print(s.at(1))
    return 0
}
